// bubbler profile: keepassxc
//
// A password manager needs a display and the database. That is what this
// grants: no network, no bus, no runtime directory shared with anything.
//
// No `network`: KeePassXC needs none to open a database. Downloading a
// site's favicon into an entry is the one feature that wants one; add
// `network` yourself if you use it, and understand that it is your
// password database's process reaching the internet.
//
// No `own "org.freedesktop.secrets"`. That rule makes this sandbox the
// Secret Service for the whole session: every application that stores a
// password through libsecret stores it here. It is an outward grant
// rather than a confinement (see the README), and first owner wins, so a
// host keyring already holding the name only makes the request fail. Add
// it, inside a `dbus` block, if you mean to replace that keyring:
//     own "org.freedesktop.secrets"
//
// No `hidraw`, and it would not help: KeePassXC reaches a YubiKey
// through libusb (/dev/bus/usb plus /sys/bus/usb) and a smart card
// through pcsclite — the binary links both libraries and holds no
// hidraw string at all. bubbler has no raw-USB grant yet, so
// challenge-response unlocking does not work from this sandbox; see
// "Known gaps".
//
// The database lives in the private home unless you share the directory
// holding it; `home-share "Documents" mode=rw` is the starting point
// below, and a database on another disk is named explicitly, e.g.
//     path-share "/kioxia/keepass" mode=rw
//
//
// `userns "disable"`: KeePassXC nests no sandbox of its own, so the door a
// nested user namespace opens stays shut.
// Bare by design. Add:
//   app-runtime "org.keepassxc.KeePassXC" mode=rw
//   lint-allow "app-runtime-rw" reason="this is the side that serves the browser socket"
//     — browser integration. KeePassXC serves
//       `org.keepassxc.KeePassXC.BrowserServer` in
//       $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/app/org.keepassxc.KeePassXC, which is the same
//       path on the host and in every sandbox naming that id, so a
//       browser on the host or in its own bubbler instance reaches it.
//       `mode=rw` because this is the side that creates the socket; the
//       browser side wants the default `ro`. Everything else granted
//       that id can replace what is in the directory, so treat the id as
//       one trust domain: KeePassXC's own associate/identification keys
//       are what authenticate a client, not the socket.
//   dbus {
//       own "org.keepassxc.KeePassXC.*"
//       talk "org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver"
//   }
//     — the org.keepassxc.KeePassXC.MainWindow interface a second
//       `keepassxc` and the desktop reach this one through, and the
//       screensaver's ActiveChanged signal, which is how "lock databases
//       when the session is locked" hears about the lock.
//   portals
//     — the file chooser that reaches outside the shared directories,
//       for a database or a key file kept elsewhere. Needs `dbus`.
//   notify
//     — the "database saved" and "entry copied" notifications. Needs
//       `dbus`.
//   tray
//     — the tray icon, and with it "minimise to tray" instead of exit.
//       Needs `dbus`.
wayland
home-share "Documents" mode=rw
userns "disable"
desktop "org.keepassxc.KeePassXC.desktop"
command "keepassxc"
